---
/* /accessibility: must stay true of the code and the test suites. */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/pages/legal.css';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';

import { site } from '../data/site';
import { plainText, wordSpans } from '../lib/words';

const heading = `Built to be used <b>by everyone.</b>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="How I build this site to WCAG 2.2 AA, what I test it with, what it does for keyboard and screen reader users, and how to tell me if something is not working."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1 aria-label={plainText(heading)} set:html={wordSpans(heading)} />
    <p class="lede">
      I build and test this site to WCAG 2.2 AA, the current version of the accessibility standard
      most public bodies use. I last checked every page against it, one requirement at a time, on
      November 8, 2026, and automated checks run on every change. No website is ever perfectly
      accessible, so if anything is hard to use with a keyboard or a screen reader, tell me and I'll
      fix it: <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated November 8, 2026</p>
  </header>
  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="toc card" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="#the-standard">The standard</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#in-practice">In practice</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#how-its-checked">How it is checked</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#where-it-stops">Where it stops short</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#report">Found a problem?</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="the-standard">The standard</h2>
      <p>
        This site is built to WCAG 2.2 AA, the current version of the standard most public bodies
        and larger organizations are held to. Each page is built to score 100 on the accessibility
        check in Lighthouse, Google's free testing tool, and an automated check runs on every change
        so that score does not quietly slip. That automated score is a useful floor, not a
        certificate: it catches the machine-checkable issues, which are only one part of the
        picture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Version 2.2 added a few rules, and this site meets each one. A link or button you reach with
        the keyboard is never hidden behind the floating menu at the bottom or the name and contact
        buttons at the top. Nothing needs a drag: the before and after pictures in a case study move
        with a single tap or click, and so does the race course map. Every button and link is big
        enough to hit, at least 24 pixels each way. The ways to reach me sit in the same order on
        every page. The contact form never asks for the same thing twice, and you can check and
        change every answer before it sends. There are no logins on this site, so the rule about
        signing in does not apply here.
      </p>
      <h2 id="in-practice">In practice</h2>
      <p>A few of the things that means on this site:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Everything you can do with a mouse, you can do with a keyboard. The focus outline is
          always visible, and a "Skip to content" link is the first thing you reach.
        </li>
        <li>
          Real landmarks and a clean heading order, so a screen reader can move through a page by
          region and by heading instead of reading it all top to bottom.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every form field has a visible label, uses the right input type, and announces its own
          errors rather than signalling them with color alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          Text is chosen to clear the AA contrast bar against its background. Images that carry
          meaning have descriptions; purely decorative ones are hidden from assistive tech.
        </li>
        <li>
          If your device is set to reduce motion, the animations and the smooth scrolling switch off
          automatically.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="how-its-checked">How it is checked</h2>
      <p>
        The accessibility score is a hard gate: a change cannot ship if it drops any template below
        100. On top of that, the pages are run through{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          axe-core
        </a>{' '}
        (a widely used accessibility testing engine) against every WCAG 2.2 A and AA rule it can
        check, in light and dark mode. Further tests tab through long pages to make sure nothing
        covers the focus, tap the sliders, and measure every button. Structural changes get a manual
        keyboard pass from the address bar through every control. Automated tools like these only
        cover part of WCAG; the rest is judgment, whether the alt text is actually useful, whether
        the reading order makes sense, so I treat the tooling as a safety net under the hands-on
        work, not a stand-in for it.
      </p>
      <h2 id="where-it-stops">Where it stops short</h2>
      <p>
        I aim for AA, not the stricter AAA, on purpose, in line with the W3C's own guidance that AAA
        is not meant as a blanket requirement for a whole site. A couple of AAA rules pull against a
        brand-led design, so the work targets AA and stops there by choice, not by oversight.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a line about polish, not about whether the site works. If a specific thing is
        genuinely hard for you to use, that is a bug, and the next section is how to flag it.
      </p>
      <h2 id="report">Found a problem?</h2>
      <p>
        If a page is hard to read, hard to get through, or breaks with the assistive technology you
        rely on, email <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a> and tell me what happened and what
        you were using. It reaches a real person, me, and I treat accessibility problems as real
        problems to fix, not as nice-to-haves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <Closing />
</BaseLayout>
